vscode: nothing — this is a decision about your editor, not an edit
open: NEOVIM.md

@brief
Neovim is a fork of vim from 2014. It was not a rewrite of the editing
model. It was a rewrite of everything underneath it: the terminal handling,
the job control, the plugin interface, and eventually the configuration
language.

That distinction is the whole chapter, so read the next sentence twice.
Every motion, operator, text object, count, register, mark, macro and ex
command in this dojo works identically in Neovim. `ci"`, `daw`, `.`,
`qq...q@q`, `:g/pat/normal A;`, `:%s//\=`, `"0p`, `Ctrl-O` — all of it,
unchanged, same keys, same semantics. Nothing you have spent thirty
chapters building is at risk. What changes is the machinery around the
editing: how you configure it, what ships in the box, and what the plugin
ecosystem expects.

This whole chapter is graded by running plain vim. Neovim is not installed
here, and it does not need to be — you will be reading and writing Lua
config files as files, the way you would write them before ever starting
nvim. Nothing in this chapter runs the Lua. Getting the translation right
on paper is the skill; typing `nvim` afterwards is the easy part.

The notes file in front of you has one bullet filed under the wrong
heading. Editing keys are not new to you, so this one is about reading.
Move it to the end of the list where it belongs.

@keys
| `dd` | cut the line |
| `G` | last line of the file |
| `p` | put it back below the cursor |

@hints
Read both headings first. One of the six things in the top list is not a
change at all — it is the thing Neovim most carefully did not change.
--
`dd` on that line, then get to the bottom of the file.
--
`G` then `p` puts the cut line after the last line.

@solution
    /Every motion<Enter> dd G p :wq<Enter>

Eighteen keystrokes, and the search is most of them.

The bullet about motions and operators belongs in the "same" list because
Neovim inherits vim's entire editing language and has never broken it.
That is not an accident of history: Neovim's stated goal was to change the
implementation without changing the user's fingers, and the compatibility
has held for over a decade.

Keep the two lists separate in your head. When someone tells you Neovim is
"a completely different editor", they are describing the plugin ecosystem
and the config language, both of which really are different. They are not
describing `d2w`.

@start NEOVIM.md
# Neovim, in one page

## What Neovim actually changed

- Built-in LSP, so go-to-definition ships with the editor
- Tree-sitter parsing, so highlighting comes from a real parser
- Lua as a first-class configuration and plugin language
- Every motion, operator, text object, register and macro you have learned
- Floating windows, which almost every modern plugin renders into
- A usable :terminal and a job API plugins can depend on

## What is exactly the same

- The ex language: ranges, :s, :g, :normal, :sort, filters
- Buffers, windows, tabs, marks, jumps, the quickfix list
- Your muscle memory, down to the last dot-repeat

@expected NEOVIM.md
# Neovim, in one page

## What Neovim actually changed

- Built-in LSP, so go-to-definition ships with the editor
- Tree-sitter parsing, so highlighting comes from a real parser
- Lua as a first-class configuration and plugin language
- Floating windows, which almost every modern plugin renders into
- A usable :terminal and a job API plugins can depend on

## What is exactly the same

- The ex language: ranges, :s, :g, :normal, :sort, filters
- Buffers, windows, tabs, marks, jumps, the quickfix list
- Your muscle memory, down to the last dot-repeat
- Every motion, operator, text object, register and macro you have learned
